# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: Lane Four Never Drops the Pocket

**Story & Context:** The debut opens at 104 BPM with Andre playing a two-bar root-octave bass figure while Malik establishes kick, snare, conga, rototom, and cowbell. The groove continues unchanged for thirty-two bars. Ratri does not enter until a short opening appears after a descending rototom phrase; she answers it with three rebab notes, the last one sliding slightly sharp before settling.

Tessa places one clipped clavinet fourth immediately after the slide, making the pitch bend feel larger than it actually is. Dimas enters only every eighth bar with a low bass-harmonica exhale beneath the final rebab note. The arrangement develops by shortening the space between Malik’s questions and Ratri’s answers, not by adding new chords.

In the final section, the conga part becomes denser while bass and kick remain identical to the opening. Ratri climbs into a higher register but never plays across the snare. The track ends with the percussion continuing for eight bars after every pitched instrument has stopped.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Night maintenance lane marked with a large painted number 4, dark inspection pit running through the center, rebab bow crossing a cream bus-route diagram, conga heads and orange safety posts lit by work lamps. No full lyrics; optional spoken fragments limited to “lane four,” “keep the pocket,” “answer the drum,” and “do not stop the floor.”

## Track 2: Cowbell Leaves Half a Beat of Air

**Story & Context:** Malik writes the EP’s most spacious pattern at 100 BPM. A single cowbell hit lands early in the second half of every bar, followed by a deliberate half-beat opening. Ratri is allowed to play only inside that opening. At first she uses one short descending tone; later she fits two notes, then a fast three-note turn, pushing against the available space without crossing its boundary.

Tessa alternates between two prepared-clavinet dyads, each struck dry and allowed almost no sustain. Andre keeps the bass line nearly static while Dimas performs low inhaled and exhaled reed textures between phrases. Halfway through, Malik removes the cowbell but leaves its empty slot intact. Ratri continues entering at exactly the place where the absent bell used to be, making the listener hear a rhythmic object that is no longer sounding.

The track closes when the cowbell returns for one final hit and Ratri deliberately does not answer it.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Close documentary image of a brass cowbell suspended above a grease-stained transit workbench, a hand-written half-beat gap marked on staff paper, rebab strings and prepared clavinet keys in shallow focus. Spoken fragments limited to “half a beat,” “leave the air,” “the bell is gone,” and “do not answer the last one.”

## Track 3: Route Clock Keeps Running After Dispatch

**Story & Context:** The closer begins with an old depot dispatch clock clicking above an uninterrupted 108 BPM percussion loop. Andre and Malik lock into a groove designed to run without structural cadence. Tessa plays a repeating clavinet fifth every four bars while Ratri uses increasingly long bowed phrases, testing how far she can stretch without covering the percussion windows.

Dimas introduces a low bass-harmonica bend that starts below Andre’s tonic and rises slowly toward it, but the bass changes root just before the reed reaches pitch. The same event happens three times, turning failed arrival into the track’s harmonic device.

During the final two minutes, one instrument disappears every sixteen bars: first clavinet, then bass harmonica, then electric bass. Malik’s percussion continues alone while Ratri keeps entering short bowed fragments between the conga cells. She finally stops. The depot clock keeps clicking over the percussion for four bars before Malik signals the ensemble to end with a single muted cowbell strike rather than a crash.

